\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TOblObjControlExtrapSent
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item OPENTOSENT:\\
x1 do [persuade x2] yesterday [x2 to go to the States next year] $\rightarrow$
x1 do [persuade x2] yesterday [to go to the States next year]
\item open QSENT:\\
x1 do [hesitate] yesterday [whether x1 to go there] $\rightarrow$
x1 do [hesitate] yesterday [whether to go there]\\
x1 do [ask himself] yesterday [where x1 to go for a holiday this year] $
\rightarrow$ x1 do [ask himself] yesterday [where to go for a holiday this year
]
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_Controlrules
\item[* date of creation:] 4-MAR-1987 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch TNOC1
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To interpret the subject of extraposed complement sentences as
identical to the direct object or the indirect object of the clause, and to
delete it. The subject must be a VAR, and is not allowed to be an EMPTY.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] One for NPVAR, one
for CNVAR. 
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
